### 4. TEACHING EXAMPLES

**Example 1 — Basic: one of each**

```javascript
let title = "JavaScript";   
let year = 1995;            
let isPopular = true;       
let nothingYet;             
let empty = null;          
let bigId = 12345678901234567890n; 
let key = Symbol("key");    
let info = { name: "JS" };  

console.log(typeof title, typeof year, typeof isPopular);
console.log(typeof nothingYet, typeof empty);
console.log(typeof bigId, typeof key, typeof info);
```

Output:
```
string number boolean
undefined object
bigint symbol object
```

**Example 2 — Slightly different: number vs numeric string**

```javascript
let a = 10;
let b = "10";
console.log(typeof a);
console.log(typeof b);   
console.log(a + a);      
console.log(b + b);     
```

Teaching point: same characters on screen, different types, different behaviour of `+`.

**Example 3 — Interview-oriented: the exceptions**

```javascript
console.log(typeof null);        // object
console.log(typeof NaN);         // number
console.log(typeof [1, 2]);      // object
console.log(typeof typeof 1);    // string
console.log(typeof undeclaredVar); // undefined
```

Teaching point: five lines that appear in interviews repeatedly. Each has a one-sentence reason (see 3.6, 3.3, 3.9, 3.10).

**Example 4 — Practical scenario: modelling a user**

```javascript
const userName = "Priya";          // string
const age = 29;                    // number
const isVerified = false;          // boolean
let lastLogin = null;              // null: never logged in yet (intentional)
let nickname;                      // undefined: not collected yet
const accountId = 98765432109876543210n; // bigint: too large for number

console.log("Name:", userName, "| type:", typeof userName);
console.log("Age:", age, "| type:", typeof age);
console.log("Verified:", isVerified, "| type:", typeof isVerified);
console.log("Last login:", lastLogin, "| type:", typeof lastLogin);
console.log("Nickname:", nickname, "| type:", typeof nickname);
console.log("Account:", accountId, "| type:", typeof accountId);
```

Teaching point: choosing types deliberately. `null` for "known to be empty", `undefined` for "not yet provided", BigInt for large IDs.

